<!-- COMMON MISTAKES -->
<section class="content" id="common-mistakes" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Pitfalls in NZST to EST Conversions</h2>
  <article>
    <h3>1. Forgetting the Calendar Day Shift</h3>
    <p>
      The single biggest error is ignoring the International Date Line. A call scheduled for Tuesday morning in New Zealand occurs on Monday afternoon in North America. Always confirm the calendar day.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>2. Confusing NZST with NZDT</h3>
    <p>
      NZST is UTC+12 (winter), while NZDT is UTC+13 (summer). Using winter offsets during summer months introduces a 1-hour error into event schedules.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>3. Conflating EST with EDT</h3>
    <p>
      EST is UTC-5 (winter), while EDT is UTC-4 (summer). Always check if North America is currently observing EST or EDT on your query date.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>4. Chatham Islands Offset Oversights</h3>
    <p>
      The Chatham Islands operate on Chatham Time (CHAST, UTC+12:45), which is 45 minutes ahead of mainland New Zealand.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

<!-- TECHNICAL TIMEZONE DATABASE SECTION -->
<section class="content" id="technical-details" aria-labelledby="tech-heading">
  <h2 id="tech-heading">Technical Specifications & IANA Timezone Database Metadata</h2>
  <p>
    Software developers require canonical IANA database keys to calculate timestamp arithmetic correctly across transpacific applications.
  </p>
  <div class="method-grid">
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>Canonical Identifiers</h3>
      <p>The primary IANA time zone key for New Zealand is <code>Pacific/Auckland</code>. The Chatham Islands key is <code>Pacific/Chatham</code>.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>ISO 8601 Offset Format</h3>
      <p>NZST is formatted in ISO 8601 strings as <code>+12:00</code> (e.g., <code>2026-07-30T10:00:00+12:00</code>).</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>POSIX Timezone Specification</h3>
      <p>The standard POSIX string for New Zealand Time is <code>NZST-12NZDT,M9.5.0,M4.1.0/3</code>, defining exact transition rules.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>NTP Atomic Clock Synchronization</h3>
      <p>Network Time Protocol (NTP) servers in New Zealand synchronize against atomic frequency standards maintained by the Measurement Standards Laboratory of New Zealand (MSL).</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

